<bx:output>
<h1>Configuration</h1>
<p class="bx-docs-lede">boxlang.json, the --bx-config flag, environment variable interpolation, and app-level settings.</p>

<h2>boxlang.json: global vs. project</h2>
<p>BoxLang's CLI only auto-loads one config file: <code class="bx-inline">~/.boxlang/config/boxlang.json</code>, the machine-wide config. A <code class="bx-inline">boxlang.json</code> placed next to your entry script is <strong>not</strong> discovered automatically — this was confirmed directly by dumping <code class="bx-inline">getBoxRuntime().getConfiguration()</code> from inside a project with its own <code class="bx-inline">boxlang.json</code>: the project file's settings were absent until passed explicitly.</p>
<table class="bx-table">
  <tr><th>Location</th><th>Auto-loaded?</th><th>Scope</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">~/.boxlang/config/boxlang.json</code></td><td>Yes, always</td><td>Every BoxLang process on the machine</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">./boxlang.json</code> (project-local)</td><td>No — requires <code class="bx-inline">--bx-config</code></td><td>Only when explicitly passed</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Using --bx-config</h2>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bash</span><code>boxlang --bx-config ./boxlang.json app.bxs</code></pre>
<p>Other useful global CLI flags (from <code class="bx-inline">boxlang --help</code>):</p>
<table class="bx-table">
  <tr><th>Flag</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">--bx-config &lt;PATH&gt;</code></td><td>Use a custom configuration file</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">--bx-home &lt;PATH&gt;</code></td><td>Set the BoxLang runtime home directory</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">--bx-debug</code></td><td>Enable debug mode with startup timing</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">--bx-code &lt;CODE&gt;</code></td><td>Execute inline BoxLang code, no file needed</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Environment variable interpolation</h2>
<p>Any BoxLang config file supports <code class="bx-inline">${env.VARIABLE_NAME:defaultValue}</code> placeholders — the default is used when the environment variable is unset, so secrets and per-environment values never need to be hardcoded:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">json</span><code>{
  "datasources": {
    "main": {
      "driver": "mysql",
      "host": "${env.MYSQL_HOST:localhost}",
      "port": "${env.MYSQL_PORT:3306}",
      "database": "${env.MYSQL_DATABASE:myapp}",
      "username": "${env.MYSQL_USERNAME:root}",
      "password": "${env.MYSQL_PASSWORD}"
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
<p>Other built-in placeholders available in any BoxLang config file: <code class="bx-inline">${boxlang-home}</code>, <code class="bx-inline">${user-home}</code>, <code class="bx-inline">${user-dir}</code>, and <code class="bx-inline">${java-temp}</code>.</p>

<h2>.env files</h2>
<p>Unlike <code class="bx-inline">boxlang.json</code>, a <code class="bx-inline">.env</code> file in the current working directory <strong>is</strong> loaded automatically — no flag needed. This was confirmed directly: a script run from a directory containing a <code class="bx-inline">.env</code> with <code class="bx-inline">MY_VAR=value</code> saw it via <code class="bx-inline">System.getenv("MY_VAR")</code>; the same script run one directory over, with no <code class="bx-inline">.env</code> present, didn't.</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">plain — .env</span><code>MYSQL_HOST=localhost
MYSQL_PORT=3306
MYSQL_DATABASE=myapp
MYSQL_USERNAME=root
MYSQL_PASSWORD=super-secret</code></pre>
<p>This is what the <code class="bx-inline">${env.VARIABLE_NAME:defaultValue}</code> placeholders above actually resolve against day to day — a <code class="bx-inline">.env</code> file per environment (gitignored, never committed) is the natural way to supply real values locally without exporting shell variables or hardcoding secrets into <code class="bx-inline">boxlang.json</code> itself.</p>
<p>A real OS environment variable of the same name always wins over the <code class="bx-inline">.env</code> file's value — confirmed the same way, by setting <code class="bx-inline">MY_VAR</code> in the shell before running a script whose <code class="bx-inline">.env</code> set a different value for it, and seeing the shell's value win. <code class="bx-inline">.env</code> only fills in what isn't already set, same as the standard dotenv convention everywhere else — safe to layer under CI/production environments that already export real values.</p>

<h2>Adding other modules</h2>
<p>BoxLang Express is itself just a BoxLang module — the same module system installs a broader ecosystem of modules beyond it. The datasource example above is a good example of where this comes up: things like additional JDBC driver support, security/encoding helpers, or a CFML compatibility layer all ship as separate, opt-in modules rather than being baked into the runtime.</p>
<p>The standard tool for this is <a href="https://commandbox.ortusbooks.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CommandBox</a> (the <code class="bx-inline">box</code> CLI) — it talks to <a href="https://forgebox.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ForgeBox</a>, the BoxLang/CFML package registry:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bash</span><code>box install bx-mysql</code></pre>
<p>A few modules you'll commonly see alongside a BoxLang project:</p>
<table class="bx-table">
  <tr><th>Module</th><th>What it's for</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">bx-mysql</code></td><td>MySQL JDBC driver support</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">bx-esapi</code></td><td>OWASP ESAPI-backed security/encoding utilities</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">bx-compat-cfml</code></td><td>Adobe/Lucee CFML compatibility layer</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Unlike a typical CommandBox package, a BoxLang module installs into <code class="bx-inline">~/.boxlang/modules/</code> — <strong>global, machine-wide</strong> — by default, since that's where the BoxLang runtime itself looks. Pass <code class="bx-inline">--local</code> to install it at the project level instead:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bash</span><code>box install bx-mysql --local</code></pre>
<p>A local install lands in <code class="bx-inline">boxlang_modules/</code>, and if there's no <code class="bx-inline">box.json</code> yet, <code class="bx-inline">box install</code> creates one and records the dependency — so a teammate (or CI) cloning the project can run <code class="bx-inline">box install</code> with no arguments to fetch everything it depends on.</p>
<div class="bx-callout bx-warn">
  <strong>Same resolution rules as boxlang-express itself</strong>
  A module installed this way is subject to the same <code class="bx-inline">boxlang_modules/</code> vs. <code class="bx-inline">~/.boxlang/modules/</code> lookup — and the same gotcha where a global copy of the same name can silently win. See <a href="/docs/getting-started">Getting Started</a> for the details.
</div>

<h3>Working against a cloned copy of a module</h3>
<p>When you're actively developing a module yourself rather than installing a released version from ForgeBox, a symlink is the most reliable option — either project-local:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bash</span><code>mkdir -p boxlang_modules
ln -s /path/to/the-module boxlang_modules/the-module</code></pre>
<p>...or machine-wide, so every project on your machine picks up the same working copy (this is what a global "npm link"-style workflow looks like for BoxLang):</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bash</span><code>ln -s /path/to/the-module ~/.boxlang/modules/the-module</code></pre>
<p>Either way, the same global-wins-over-local resolution above still applies — a machine-wide symlink is what actually resolves if both exist for the same module name.</p>

<h2>App-level settings</h2>
<p>Separate from the CLI/runtime config above, BoxLang Express has its own small settings bag on the <code class="bx-inline">app</code> object, set with <code class="bx-inline">app.set(name, value)</code> and read back with <code class="bx-inline">app.getSetting(name)</code>:</p>
<table class="bx-table">
  <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">"views"</code></td><td>Directory <code class="bx-inline">res.render()</code> resolves view files from. See <a href="/docs/views">Views &amp; Templates</a>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">"env"</code></td><td>When set to <code class="bx-inline">"development"</code>, the default 500 handler exposes the real error message instead of a generic one. Custom error middleware can read this the same way — see <a href="/docs/errors">Error Handling</a>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">"trust proxy"</code></td><td>Which proxies to believe for <code class="bx-inline">req.ip</code>, <code class="bx-inline">req.protocol</code> and <code class="bx-inline">req.hostname</code>. A number trusts that many hops (<code class="bx-inline">1</code> for a single load balancer); a list of addresses or CIDR ranges trusts those proxies; <code class="bx-inline">true</code> takes the left-most <code class="bx-inline">X-Forwarded-For</code> entry, which a client can forge when its proxy appends to the header — prefer a number or a list. Off by default.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">"requestTimeoutMs"</code></td><td>Answers <code class="bx-inline">503</code> when a handler hasn't started its response in this many milliseconds. Off by default. See <a href="##limits">Timeouts and size limits</a>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">"etag"</code></td><td><code class="bx-inline">"weak"</code>, <code class="bx-inline">"strong"</code> or a function to tag buffered responses, so repeat requests can be answered with <code class="bx-inline">304</code>. Off by default — see <a href="/docs/request-response##etags">ETags and 304s</a></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">"maxBodySize"</code></td><td>Largest request body, in bytes, for the whole app — a bigger one gets <code class="bx-inline">413</code> before any handler runs. Off by default.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">"headerTimeoutMs"</code> / <code class="bx-inline">"keepAliveTimeoutMs"</code> / <code class="bx-inline">"maxHeaderSize"</code></td><td>Connection-level limits: time to send request headers (60s), idle time between requests (60s), and total header size (64 KB).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">"reloadOnChange"</code></td><td>Dev-mode auto-restart on file change. See <a href="/docs/lifecycle">Process Lifecycle</a>.</td></tr>
</table>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.set( "env", "development" )
app.set( "trust proxy", 1 )
app.set( "views", expandPath( "./views" ) )</code></pre>

<h2 id="limits">Timeouts and size limits</h2>
<p>Five settings bound what one client can cost the server. <code class="bx-inline">0</code> turns any of them off, and a value that isn't a whole number throws at the <code class="bx-inline">app.set()</code> call.</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.set( "requestTimeoutMs", 30000 )   // a handler has 30s to start responding
app.set( "maxBodySize", 12582912 )     // 12MB, a little over the largest upload this app accepts</code></pre>
<p>Both lines are in this site's own <code class="bx-inline">app.bxs</code>. When a request runs past its timeout, the handler's thread is interrupted — so the query, HTTP call or <code class="bx-inline">sleep()</code> it's waiting on throws — and the client gets a <code class="bx-inline">503</code>. One route that needs a different limit sets its own with <code class="bx-inline">req.setTimeout( ms )</code>:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.get( "/docs/config/demo/slow", ( req, res ) => {
    req.setTimeout( 1000 )   // this request only: 1s instead of the app's 30s
    sleep( 5000 )            // stands in for a query that never comes back
    res.send( "you won't see this" )
} )</code></pre>
<p><a href="/docs/config/demo/slow">Try it</a> — the response arrives after about one second, not five:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bash</span><code>curl -i http://localhost:3003/docs/config/demo/slow
## HTTP/1.1 503 Service Unavailable
## Connection: close
##
## {"error":true,"message":"Request timed out"}</code></pre>
<div class="bx-callout">
  <strong>What a timeout can and can't stop</strong>
  The timeout covers the time to <em>start</em> a response, so an SSE stream or a large download isn't cut off, and <code class="bx-inline">req.setTimeout( 0 )</code> exempts a route entirely. A handler that never blocks — a pure CPU loop — has nothing to interrupt and isn't stopped. Error-handling middleware doesn't run for a timeout; a handler that catches the exception itself can check <code class="bx-inline">req.timedOut</code> and answer however it likes.
</div>
<p><code class="bx-inline">"maxBodySize"</code> sits above the body parsers' own <code class="bx-inline">limit</code> options, which only apply where a parser is mounted. A request that declares its size is refused on its <code class="bx-inline">Content-Length</code> header alone, before the body is read:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bash</span><code>head -c 13000000 /dev/zero | curl -i -X POST --data-binary @- http://localhost:3003/upload
## HTTP/1.1 413 Request Entity Too Large</code></pre>

<div class="bx-docs-pager">
  <a href="/docs/getting-started"><span>Previous</span>← Getting Started</a>
  <a href="/docs/routing"><span>Next</span>Routing →</a>
</div>
</bx:output>
